Both arms shifted: tipping point delta = -7.31.
\image(figures/tipping_point.svg)
// result_end

Under MAR the pooled treatment effect is 2.49 points (p = 0.017). The conclusion is fragile in the direction that matters. If active-arm dropouts did on average only 1.7 points worse than MAR predicts, about a fifth of the outcome SD, the effect would no longer be significant at 5%. Because only a quarter of the arm is shifted, the estimate falls by about a quarter of \(\delta\) per point, and it reaches zero only at \(\delta\approx-9.9\). Whether a 1.7-point penalty for dropping out of active treatment is plausible is a clinical question. Discontinuing patients often lose the benefit of treatment, so a shift of the size of the treatment effect itself (here 3 points) is a natural benchmark, and it lies beyond the tipping point.

Shifting dropouts in both arms by the same amount barely moves the estimate: the bias largely cancels. The small decline comes from the slightly higher dropout rate and different baseline mix in the active arm, and significance survives up to \(\delta\approx-7.3\). This is why sensitivity analyses that shift all dropouts are uninformative about the treatment effect. The departures that matter are differential between arms, as in "jump to reference" and similar reference-based assumptions.

The fraction of missing information falls slightly as \(\delta\) grows. The shift is a constant added to the imputations and adds no between-imputation variance, while the within-imputation variance grows a little as imputed values move away from the observed ones. Reusing the seed makes the estimate exactly linear in \(\delta\), which the unit tests verify, and makes the interpolated tipping point reproducible.

>> Practical guidance

- Choose the delta scale and direction with clinicians before seeing the results, and express it in outcome units or SD units.
- Shift the arm and pattern whose departure from MAR would favor the treatment, usually active-arm dropouts after discontinuation. Equal shifts in both arms answer a different and less relevant question.
- Report the tipping point together with the MAR result and a judgment of plausibility. A tipping point far beyond credible departures supports robustness.
- Use enough imputations (here 20) and a fixed seed, so that the tipping point is not dominated by Monte Carlo error.
- For several incomplete variables, remember that the shift propagates through the chained equations. Shift only the outcome whose missingness is in question.

>> References

- J. R. Carpenter and M. G. Kenward (2013). *Multiple Imputation and its Application*. Wiley, chapter 10.
- National Research Council (2010). *The Prevention and Treatment of Missing Data in Clinical Trials*. National Academies Press. doi:10.17226/12955
- G. Molenberghs and M. G. Kenward (2007). *Missing Data in Clinical Studies*. Wiley.
- J. R. Carpenter, J. H. Roger and M. G. Kenward (2013). Analysis of longitudinal trials with protocol deviation: a framework for relevant, accessible assumptions, and inference via multiple imputation. *Journal of Biopharmaceutical Statistics* 23(6), 1352-1371. doi:10.1080/10543406.2013.834911
